    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 298.434, 299.083, 319.854 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "See Figure 1 for a schematic representation of the experiment."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 17,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 149.493, 299.085, 296.94 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The discrepancy between the expected and the reported initial and final states may be due to large deviations, i.e., obtaining an unlikely ensemble from a collection of measurements due the finite size of the experimental record, or due to pre- and post-selection, where our assistant might have chosen a sub-ensemble of realizations to cook up some desired α˜i and β˜j probabilities. Either way, in the spirit of Schr¨dinger’s original gedanken experiment, we pose the following question: what is the most likely joint probability p˜ij that led to the outcomes ρ˜0 and ρ˜1? In other words, if the outcomes were postselected, what is the most likely way this post-selection was achieved?"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 18,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 103.591, 116.738, 249.487, 125.705 ],
    "font" : "CMBX9",
    "font size" : 8.966,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "B. A large deviations solution"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 19,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 67.945, 299.082, 100.822 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The question raised above reduces to a classical large deviations problem of the same nature as the one that Schr¨dinger answered in [1]. In modern terms, we seek to"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 20,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 317.014, 537.067, 562.097, 592.859 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "quantify the likelihood of unexpected outcomes through Sanov’s theorem, which states that the probability P of drawing an atypical distribution from a finite collection of N realizations decays exponentially as N → ∞. Specifically,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 21,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 416.392, 509.87, 462.718, 529.001 ],
    "font" : "CMMI10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "P ∼ e−IN,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 22,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 317.014, 450.99, 562.099, 506.781 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "where the decay rate is given by the large deviations rate function I that quantifies the distance between P and the typical probability distribution. Thereby, the most likely atypical distribution that is consistent with given outcomes is the one that minimizes the rate function."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 23,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 317.014, 393.705, 562.099, 449.496 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Although the experiment we consider involves a quantum evolution, the measurement at the two sites where pre- and post-selection takes place, render the probabilistic model of the experimental setting classical. As a result, the rate function is the relative entropy"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
